<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">United Principalities</h1><p class="st-meta">1859 CE – 1881 CE · capital Bucharest</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Union of Moldavia and Wallachia under one prince that created the modern Romanian state and won its independence from the Ottomans.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> settlement allowed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/principality-of-moldavia">Moldavia</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-wallachia">Wallachia</a> separate assemblies under a common commission. Exploiting a loophole, both assemblies elected the same man, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandru-ioan-cuza">Alexandru Ioan Cuza</a>, as prince in January and February 1859. The personal union became a single state with one government and parliament in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a> in 1862, taking the name <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-romania">Romania</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandru-ioan-cuza">Cuza</a> secularised the vast monastery estates in 1863, carried out a land reform in 1864, introduced a civil code and founded the universities of Iași and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a>. His authoritarian methods provoked a coup in 1866, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carol-i-of-romania">Carol</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-hohenzollern-sigmaringen">Hohenzollern-Sigmaringen</a> was installed as prince under a liberal constitution modelled on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium's</a>. Grain exports through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galati">Galați</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/braila">Brăila</a> drove the economy.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-romania">Romania</a> declared independence in 1877 and fought alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-berlin">Congress of Berlin</a> in 1878 recognised independence, adding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-dobruja">Northern Dobruja</a> but returning southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a> to Russia. In 1881 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carol-i-of-romania">Carol</a> was crowned king, founding the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/united-principalities.jpg" alt="United Principalities" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Alex:D · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_United_Principalities_of_Romania_(1862%E2%80%931866).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1859 CE – 1881 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bucharest</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Alexandru Ioan Cuza, Carol I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Romanian Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Romanian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>United Principalities of Moldavia and Wallachia, Principality of Romania</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1870&amp;focus=united-principalities" aria-label="United Principalities on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5304 607 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where United Principalities lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5624 863L5624 862M5624 863L5646 778M5624 863L5709 970M5624 863L5624 863"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5624 862h0M5646 778h0M5709 970h0M5624 863h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5624 863h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5624 863h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bucharest. <a href="/atlas?year=1870&amp;focus=united-principalities">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
